L. That means offering your source code to recipients under GPL terms, which a closed-source product can't do.\n- If the product is a hosted service that users never receive a copy of, plain GPL-3.0 isn't triggered. This is the \"SaaS loophole\". It doesn't apply if the license is actually AGPL, so check that. It also stops applying if you later ship an on-prem or client-side version.\n- GPL-3.0 has extra problems for locked-down devices, because of its anti-tivoization terms.\n\nYour options, in order of preference:\n1. Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0).\n2. Ask the authors whether they sell a commercial license or offer a dual license.\n3. Keep it only if it's used as a separate process (for example, a CLI you call) with no linking. This is a gray area, so get legal sign-off first.\n4. Drop the feature that needs it.\n\n**The other two**\n- **requests (Apache-2.0)** is fine. You need to include the license text and any NOTICE file, and you must not remove copyright notices.\n- **left-pad (MIT)** is fine. You need to include the copyright and license notice in your third-party notices.\n\n**Other things to check before shipping**\n- `deps.txt` only lists direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies can carry GPL or AGPL licenses too, so scan the full tree.\n- Confirm the license declared in the package matches what's in its repo. The list is only as accurate as whoever wrote it.\n- The version strings look truncated (`requests==2.32`, with no patch version). Pin exact versions and check for known CVEs.\n- Generate a third-party notices file to ship with the product.\n\nI haven't looked at how somelib is used in the code. If you want, I can check whether it's imported or linked, or scan for transitive dependencies. That would show how much the GPL issue affects you. I'm not a lawyer, so have counsel confirm the final call on somelib.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}